        "slide": 9,
        "slideId": "s9",
        "speaker": "Dr. Nova",
        "text": "Slide 9 examines a silent killer of geospatial machine learning datasets: Coordinate Reference System, or CRS, mismatches. When ingesting field telemetry from GPS probes, IoT sensors, and satellite imagery, data engineers frequently treat latitude and longitude as Cartesian coordinates. This is a fatal misconception. Latitude and longitude under WGS84, or EPSG:4326, are angular measurements in degrees on a three-dimensional oblate spheroid. In contrast, raster satellite grids, such as Sentinel-2 or Landsat, are mapped onto projected planar coordinate systems, such as Universal Transverse Mercator, or EPSG:32614, where units are meters. If you perform a naive spatial join or Euclidean distance calculation between unprojected degrees and projected meters, coordinates distort severely. In agricultural trials, this shift commonly exceeds 140 meters, mapping soil probes into entirely wrong farm parcels.",
